  "worked_solution": "Let $F(x,y)=P(x,y)\\,i+Q(x,y)\\,j$ be a vector field with domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first-order partial derivatives everywhere on $D.$ Then, we have the following theorem:\n\n$F$ *is conservative if and only if $\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}$ everywhere on $D.$*\n\nNote that for each of our functions, $D={R}^{2}.$ With this in mind, let's check each of the vector fields.  \n\n- For the vector field $F,$ we have $P=xy$ and $Q=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=x,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are continuous and equal everywhere on ${R}^{2},$ we conclude that $F$ is conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $G,$ we have $P=4x+{y}^{2}$ and $Q=-4x{y}^{2}.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=2y,\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=-4{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $G$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- For the vector field $H,$ we have $P=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy)$ and $Q=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy).$ Computing the partial derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\u2202P}{\u2202y}=x\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(xy),\\,\\frac{\u2202Q}{\u2202x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(xy),.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the partial derivatives are not equal, we conclude that $H$ is not conservative on ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
